solution_steps:
We want to find an integer $x$ such that $11x \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}$.
We can solve this congruence using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.
\begin{align*}
1000 &= 11 \cdot 90 + 10 \\
11 &= 10 \cdot 1 + 1
\end{align*}
Working backwards, we have
\begin{align*}
1 &= 11 - 10 \cdot 1 \\
&= 11 - (1000 - 11 \cdot 90) \cdot 1 \\
&= 11 \cdot 91 - 1000 \cdot 1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the modular inverse of $11$ modulo $1000$ is $\boxed{91}$.The answer is: 91

