We know that the sine function has a period of $360^\circ$, so we can write $510^\circ$ as $510^\circ - 360^\circ = 150^\circ$.
Since $150^\circ$ is in the second quadrant, $\sin 150^\circ = \sin (180^\circ - 150^\circ) = \sin 30^\circ = \boxed{\frac{1}{2}}$.
The answer is: \frac{1}{2}

Using the identity $\tan (90^\circ - x) = \frac{1}{\tan x}$, we can rewrite the expression as
\begin{align*}
\log_{10}(\tan 1^\circ) + \log_{10}(\tan 2^\circ) + \log_{10}(\tan 3^\circ) + \dots + \log_{10}(\tan 88^\circ) + \log_{10}(\tan 89^\circ) &= \log_{10}\left(\tan 1^\circ \cdot \tan 2^\circ \cdot \tan 3^\circ \cdots \tan 88^\circ \cdot \tan 89^\circ \right) \\
&= \log_{10}\left(\frac{\sin 1^\circ \cdot \sin 2^\circ \cdot \sin 3^\circ \cdots \sin 88^\circ \cdot \sin 89^\circ}{\cos 1^\circ \cdot \cos 2^\circ \cdot \cos 3^\circ \cdots \cos 88^\circ \cdot \cos 89^\circ}\right).
\end{align*}
Since $\sin (90^\circ - x) = \cos x$, the numerator and denominator cancel out, leaving us with $\log_{10}(1) = \boxed{0}$.The answer is: 0
